Provident Vehicle Detection at Night (PVDN) Project
Early Detection of Approaching Vehicles Using Reflection Analysis and Deep Learning
Project Overview

The Provident Vehicle Detection at Night (PVDN) project focuses on detecting approaching vehicles in nighttime driving scenes before the vehicle itself becomes clearly visible. Traditional vehicle detection systems usually rely on visible vehicle shapes, headlights, or taillights. However, in real nighttime environments, vehicles may still be far away, partially hidden, or not directly visible.

The main idea of this project is to teach an AI system to recognize early reflection patterns caused by approaching vehicle headlights on the road surface and surrounding environment.

Instead of asking:

“Can the system see the car?”

the project asks:

“Can the system detect the reflections that indicate a car is approaching?”

This makes the system capable of earlier warning generation in low-light conditions, which is important for intelligent transportation systems, autonomous driving, and driver assistance applications.

Dataset Used

The project uses the PVDN (Provident Vehicle Detection at Night) dataset from Kaggle.

The dataset contains:

Night driving videos
Annotated vehicle reflection points
Vehicle visibility stages
Reflection artifacts
Road scenes captured under nighttime conditions

The dataset includes:

Training set
Validation set
Test set

The annotations contain information such as:

{
   "pos": [x, y],
   "direct": false
}

Where:

pos represents the reflection position
direct: false means the vehicle itself is not directly visible yet

This is extremely important because it allows the model to learn reflection-based early vehicle detection rather than ordinary object detection.

Main Goal of the Project

The objective of the project is:

Detect approaching vehicles at night
Detect vehicles before they become visible
Reduce false positives caused by road lights and reflections
Build a real-time video-based warning system
Combine classical computer vision with deep learning

The final system acts as an intelligent nighttime monitoring pipeline that continuously analyzes video frames and predicts whether an approaching vehicle is present.

What We Did in the Project
1. Data Loading and Processing

We first loaded the PVDN dataset and analyzed:

Video frames
Reflection annotations
Vehicle stage labels
Reflection artifacts

We created custom dataset loaders using PyTorch and OpenCV to handle:

Grayscale night images
Reflection maps
Heatmap generation
Multi-task training labels

The images were resized and normalized before training.

2. Reflection Heatmap Generation

One of the earliest ideas in the project was teaching the model where reflections are likely to appear.

We generated saliency maps and Gaussian heatmaps around annotated reflection positions.

The generated heatmaps represented:

Vehicle reflection regions
Reflection artifacts
Possible vehicle appearance areas

This allowed the model to learn spatial reflection patterns instead of only learning object appearance.

3. Building the AVPNet Architecture

We designed a custom deep learning architecture called:

AVPNet (Artifact-to-Vehicle Perception Network)

The architecture was based on:

EfficientNet-B0 encoder
U-Net style decoder
Multi-task prediction heads

The network was designed to simultaneously:

Predict reflection saliency maps
Predict artifact maps
Classify vehicle approach stages

The model accepted nighttime road images and learned reflection behavior patterns.

4. Multi-Task Learning

The model was trained using multiple objectives:

Vehicle Reflection Prediction

Predict where reflections appear.

Artifact Detection

Separate real vehicle reflections from road artifacts.

Vehicle Stage Classification

Classify whether the vehicle is:

not visible
weakly visible
partially approaching
strongly approaching

This helped the model learn richer nighttime scene understanding.

5. Reflection Detection Using Classical Computer Vision

After the segmentation approach, we experimented with a rule-based reflection detector using OpenCV.

This pipeline included:

LAB color space conversion
CLAHE contrast enhancement
Adaptive thresholding
Morphological filtering
Contour extraction

The goal was to isolate bright nighttime reflection regions from the road.

This stage produced reflection proposals that could later be classified by deep learning models.

6. Patch Classification System

We then created a reflection patch classification system.

The idea was:

Crop small reflection regions
Train a CNN to classify:
vehicle reflection
non-vehicle reflection

We trained an EfficientNet-based patch classifier using curated reflection patches.

The notebook responsible for training was:

06_train_patch_classifier.ipynb

The trained model weights were saved as:

best_patch_model.pth

The classifier learned reflection appearance patterns such as:

glow structure
light intensity
reflection texture
road illumination behavior
7. Initial Sliding Window Detection (First Attempt)

The first inference system scanned the entire road frame using many overlapping patches.

Pipeline:

Frame
→ sliding windows
→ CNN classification
→ detections

However, this produced many false positives because the CNN was trained on carefully selected reflection patches, not random road textures.

As a result:

road glare
lane markings
bright asphalt
streetlights

were often incorrectly classified as vehicle reflections.

The system generated warnings in almost every frame.

Final Improved System

The final and most successful stage of the project was the improved hybrid detection pipeline.

The notebook responsible for this final implementation was:

08_blob_proposal_plus_tracking_improved.ipynb

This became the complete final vehicle detection system.

Final System Pipeline

The final system works as:

Video Frame
→ Bright Blob Proposal
→ Proposal Filtering
→ EfficientNet CNN Verification
→ Proposal Quality Scoring
→ Top Detections
→ Temporal Tracking
→ Final Vehicle Warning
Step-by-Step Explanation of the Final System
Step 1 — Video Input

The system reads nighttime driving video frame-by-frame using OpenCV.

Example:

cap = cv2.VideoCapture(VIDEO_PATH)
ret, frame = cap.read()

Each frame is processed independently and later connected through tracking.

Step 2 — Frame Preprocessing

The nighttime frame is enhanced to make reflections easier to detect.

The preprocessing stage includes:

grayscale conversion
contrast enhancement
noise reduction
threshold preparation

Conceptually:

Dark Night Frame
→ Contrast Enhancement
→ Bright Reflection Isolation

This improves weak reflection visibility.

Step 3 — Blob Proposal Generation

Instead of scanning the entire image using sliding windows, the system first searches for bright connected regions called blobs.

A blob proposal is simply:

“A region that might contain a vehicle reflection.”

Example proposal:

[x1, y1, x2, y2]

Example detection box:

[613, 473, 666, 551]

At this stage, the system does NOT yet say:

“This is definitely a vehicle.”

It only says:

“This bright region is worth checking.”

Step 4 — Proposal Filtering

The generated proposals are filtered before CNN classification.

Filtering criteria include:

brightness
position on road
geometric size
aspect ratio
reflection quality
region consistency

This removes:

street lights
lane reflections
noise
glare artifacts
unrealistic blobs

This stage significantly reduced false positives.

Step 5 — CNN Verification

Each remaining blob is:

cropped
resized
normalized
passed into the trained EfficientNet patch classifier

The CNN outputs a probability score representing:

“How likely is this region to be a vehicle reflection?”

Example:

'prob': 0.973

Meaning:

97.3% confidence that the patch resembles a vehicle reflection
Step 6 — Proposal Quality Scoring

The system does not rely only on CNN confidence.

It combines:

CNN probability
blob quality
geometric reliability

Example:

'prob': 0.973
'quality': 0.665
'score': 0.647

This ensures that detections are both:

visually correct
physically realistic
Step 7 — Top Detection Selection

Only the strongest detections are kept.

Instead of allowing many detections per frame, the system selects:

high-confidence
high-quality
spatially consistent detections

This prevents permanent warning activation.

Step 8 — Temporal Tracking

The system tracks detections across consecutive frames.

Example:

Frame 1 → reflection detected
Frame 2 → nearby reflection detected
Frame 3 → same reflection continues
Frame 4 → reflection persists

This is one of the biggest improvements in the project.

A single-frame detection is not enough anymore.

The reflection must:

persist over time
move consistently
behave like an approaching vehicle
Step 9 — Final Warning Decision

The final warning is generated only if:

the reflection is strong
the CNN confirms it
the blob quality is good
the detection persists across frames

This makes the system much more stable and reliable.

Why the Final System Worked Better

The original patch CNN only answered:

“Does this image patch look like a reflection?”

The final system answers:

“Is there a realistic reflection candidate that behaves consistently across time like an approaching vehicle?”

This is much stronger because the final system combines:

computer vision
deep learning
spatial filtering
temporal tracking
reflection behavior analysis
Main Improvements Achieved

The improved pipeline successfully:

reduced false positives
avoided random road detections
improved detection stability
improved nighttime robustness
added temporal consistency
created realistic vehicle warning behavior

Example debug improvement:

Before:
Too many detections per frame

After:
Proposals: 19
Detections: 1

This demonstrated that the proposal-based system became much cleaner and more reliable.

Technologies and Libraries Used

The project used:

Python
PyTorch
OpenCV
NumPy
Pandas
torchvision
segmentation_models_pytorch
EfficientNet
Matplotlib
Final Project Contribution

The final project is not simply a CNN classifier anymore.

It became a complete intelligent nighttime vehicle detection pipeline capable of:

analyzing video streams
detecting reflection behavior
filtering noise
tracking detections
generating reliable early warnings

The project demonstrates how combining classical computer vision with deep learning and temporal tracking can significantly improve nighttime vehicle perception systems.
